WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:09.340
I want to talk to you today
about how it is I talk to you.

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:13.580
Critical to human existence

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:16.700
is that our voices define who we are.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:19.900
My voice is me.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:21.620
Your voice is you.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:28.380
Voice is our main means of communication,
evolved over millennia.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.060
And I want to argue today

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:35.820
that there are three
vocal resiliences for humans.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:40.620
We need our voices
for all kinds of things.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:45.580
We transmit verbal information,
ideas, feelings, emotions.

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:49.860
But more to the point, our identity.

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:52.660
You answer the phone,

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:54.700
and very often you recognize somebody

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:57.380
before they've even said
what their name is.

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:02.060
It's also the way we call
for attention if we're in trouble.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:04.380
And of course,

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:08.420
it also provides the lyrics in singing.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:15.620
So voice is fundamental to human living.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:18.220
In speech,

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:20.020
in different acoustic situations,

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:21.860
in the presence of competing sounds

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:23.860
and in song,

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:25.900
over millennia.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:31.580
And part of what I want to say today
reflects on the millennial part of this.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:35.060
But first, let's look
at the three resiliencies.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:40.140
And before we do,

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:43.340
I want just briefly
to think about the role of hearing,

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:48.740
particularly the role
of mankind's creation of devices

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:52.460
that can play very loud sounds.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:57.580
Sounds that are louder
than any human voice can produce

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:00.260
through the application of electronics.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:05.100
So over roughly the last 130 years,

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:07.140
we have the potential

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:12.260
to break the human
communication voice-to-ear train

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:18.420
because we are damaging hearing
over just a century and a bit,

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:22.780
having evolved over millennia,
our communication system.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.660
So these are my three resiliences.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:31.540
The first is resilience to other sounds
in the environment.

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:38.380
The second is allowing us
one-to-many communication.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:43.100
And the third is to preserve
the voice over millennia.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:45.140
But I want us to remember,

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:50.380
there is little resistance
to loud human-made sounds.

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:54.140
And that, I would argue,
is an issue that's getting worse.

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:58.460
We need to act to both
understand the problem

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:01.140
and to protect our hearing.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:08.060
Many of us carry devices around
which can cause hearing difficulty

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:09.940
if you play them too loud.

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:12.540
And hearing issues, of course,

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:15.660
these are not on
an evolutionary time scale.

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:18.620
These are on a very short time scale.

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:20.220
So here's the first one,

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:22.380
other sounds in the environment,

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:23.780
sounds of nature.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:30.500
We have evolved a redundancy
in our speech specifically

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:34.180
that allows us to hear speech
in the presence of natural sound.

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:37.420
So if, for example, there's a thunderstorm

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:39.980
or there's heavy rain,

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:43.420
then there is what we call
masking in the sound.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:45.900
And I'm going to do a little experiment.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:51.020
I want to demonstrate
that you can understand my voice

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:55.220
even if we cut the high end off
or cut the low end off.

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:57.580
So the way we're going to do this

00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:01.100
is we're going to listen
to the low end first.

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:04.980
And I'm hoping that my microphone
is connected now to a filter

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:10.180
as I give you a demonstration
of just the low end.

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:12.620
And I hope you can still understand me.

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:16.740
And now would you switch it
so we only hear the high end,

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:18.700
a so-called high-pass filter?

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:23.540
And now, I hope
you can still understand me,

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:25.900
even though I sound a bit sibilant.

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:31.300
I hope you all understood
what I was saying.

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:33.660
Now, that means

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:36.460
that if you're only able
to hear the low end

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:40.700
because of some high frequency noise
that's in the environment,

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:43.900
we can still communicate as human beings.

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:45.100
And vice versa.

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:48.340
If you can only hear the high end
because there's rumbles of thunder

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:49.660
and other things going on,

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:52.300
we can still communicate.

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:55.540
It's a wonder of vocal evolution.

00:04:56.980 --> 00:04:58.300
Resilience two.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.980
Now, I have to admit, I'm cheating.

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:04.820
I am talking one-to-many,

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:06.580
and I'm wearing this thing

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:09.500
and you can hear me
because of the loudspeakers.

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:13.420
But if I was an opera singer
on this stage,

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:14.860
I wouldn't have a microphone,

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:17.100
I'd have an orchestra between me and you

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:19.780
and loads of people in the auditorium.

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:26.340
And yet, you can hear the words
of the opera singer without a microphone,

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:27.980
without loudspeakers.

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:30.180
And the way they do it

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:33.460
is they engage that aspect
of the human voice,

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:35.300
which I won't use over a microphone.

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:38.420
And the way that works is this.

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:43.100
The voice box or the larynx,
the picture in the middle,

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:45.900
is here in the neck,
it's where the Adam's apple is.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:47.780
And of course, we have two ears.

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:51.620
When I do that kind of sound,

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:53.780
I create a narrow tube,

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:57.540
as shown by the light blue arrow
in the larynx area.

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:04.340
And that tube matches in dimensions

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:07.620
the tube here and the tube here.

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:10.020
So in engineering terms,

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:14.140
we set up a transmitter and a receiver

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:16.900
that are tuned to work together.

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:20.900
And again, that is an evolved way

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:24.900
in which the larynx has developed
to allow us to do it.

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:28.220
And you don't have to be
a trained opera singer.

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:32.100
If you're in danger,

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:34.700
you know how to do this

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:36.300
because it's a natural,

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:40.380
something that's stored
in the brain from evolution,

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:44.340
which you can switch in
in time of real need.

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:47.220
Let me go to the third one.

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:49.300
Now, this is the one
that's been alluded to,

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:52.460
was the question that our group asked:

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:57.060
Can we recreate the sound
of a 3,000-year-old mummy?

00:06:57.100 --> 00:06:58.580
It's an interesting question,

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:01.020
and the answer is that we can

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:04.940
if we can recreate the tube
between the larynx and the lips,

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:07.860
because that's the tube,
the so-called vocal tract,

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:09.300
the mouth and throat,

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:11.980
that I'm using now as I speak to you.

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:14.580
And here is the very image.

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:17.580
This is a 3D plastic vocal tract.

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:21.260
And if I put it next to mine,
you can see it’s about the right shape.

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:24.140
And we then put this on
an artificial larynx,

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:28.460
which is the loudspeaker in the picture,
that's two in from the top.

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:32.100
We can then play a sound
through the loudspeaker.

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:35.060
And we get the sound of this vowel.

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:36.220
(Sound)

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:39.060
Of course, it's not speech

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:42.620
because to speak,
I have to move my vocal tract,

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:44.940
I have to move the articulators.

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:46.740
And this, of course, is solid.

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:50.180
But in this particular case, Nesyamun,

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:53.060
the hieroglyphics shown
on the right there,

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:57.620
in English, that hieroglyphic
means true of voice.

00:07:57.940 --> 00:08:00.140
And Nesyamun wrote

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:05.220
that he expected his voice
to be heard in the afterlife.

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:11.380
So this work was not just a technical
“Can we make the sound?”

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:16.060
It also had a message,
particularly to Egyptologists,

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:18.060
who study mummies,

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:20.620
of something rather special.

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:26.460
So we are hearing a voice
from three millennia ago.

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:31.220
So as I started, my voice is me.

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:32.660
Your voice is you.

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:37.780
Our modern, noisier world is a challenge.

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:42.900
And it's a challenge
from the last 100 to 150 years.

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:46.900
And it's something
that we need to think about.

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:51.100
We need to think about it in terms
of the numbers of humans

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:55.220
who are getting hearing problems
because of the noise around us.

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:58.900
And if we're going to thrive as humans,

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:01.140
we need to communicate with each other

00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:04.620
and we need our voices to do that.

00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:11.500
And I've suggested three areas
of vocal resilience.

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:15.700
So please, look after yours.

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:16.900
Thank you.

00:09:16.900 --> 00:09:19.980
(Applause)

